o an entire gender, you're no longer making a private observation — you're institutionalising a bias. That's a step up in seriousness, even if the stereotype behind it feels mild. To your broader point: I'm not "hung up" because I think this is the worst harm in the world. I'm persistent because you asked whether you should adopt it as a formal approach, and the answer is *no, that will backfire on you professionally and practically*. I'd give the same persistence on any training question where someone wanted to adopt a stereotype — even a common one — as a design strategy. It's not about policing your thoughts; it's about helping you create the most effective, defensible training you can. So feel free to laugh at the family stories and the sitcoms — they exist for a reason. But keep them out of your materials, not because you're a bad person if you relate to them, but because they make your training weaker, not stronger. That's the simple, practical bottom line.
